# .xal/gate-inputs — the declared input contract for scripts/check.sh (<SERVICE>).
#
# Every gate in scripts/check.sh that needs something NOT in this repo declares it here, and
# .xal/check-gate-inputs.sh asserts that every workflow invoking that script supplies it.
# This file exists because a gate script cannot see its own callers: a drift gate once
# gained a sibling checkout, ci.yml got it, deploy.yml did not, and production deploys were
# blocked for ~3.5h while CI on the same commit stayed green. See
# docs/process/gate-discipline.md §4.
#
# Format:  id | detect | required-in-ci | caller-pattern | expires | description
#   detect          command:<name> | env:<VAR> | none   (how check.sh discovers it)
#   required-in-ci  yes = the gate FAILS when absent under CI=true; no = it skips
#   caller-pattern  ERE every caller workflow must match, `<workflow>:<ERE>` for one
#                   workflow only, or `-` for runner-provided
#   expires         ISO date YYYY-MM-DD, or `-` for an input that cannot expire. A
#                   caller-pattern naming `secrets.` must carry a real date: the checker
#                   refuses `-` there, because a secret has an expiry whether or not it
#                   is written down.
#
# Adding a gate that needs an input and NOT adding it here fails the gate-inputs check
# locally, before push. That is the point: declare it, then wire every caller. Both halves,
# or neither counts.
#
# When this service gains a credential (a private checkout, a deploy token), add a row with
# a REAL expiry date. A placeholder date fails gate 0 until replaced — deliberately.

go-toolchain   | none                 | yes | \./\.github/actions/toolchain | -     | The Go toolchain pinned by go.mod's `toolchain` directive. Every gate that shells out to `go` or `gofmt` (modules, format, vet, lint, test, coverage, audit) needs it and nothing detects it. SUPPLIED BY THE ONE COMPOSITE ACTION every caller uses (`.github/actions/toolchain/action.yml` holds setup-go and both pinned installs), so the caller pattern is the action's path: a workflow that calls the action supplies all three rows at once, and a workflow that pastes the steps inline instead fails this row, deliberately, because the driver re-runs the ACTION after the agent and scripts/driver/step-order.sh cannot vouch for inline steps.
golangci-lint  | command:golangci-lint | yes | \./\.github/actions/toolchain | -     | The aggregate linter for the lint gate. Installed by the toolchain action at a pinned version; never `@latest`, because a linter that changes under you turns an unrelated PR red.
govulncheck    | command:govulncheck  | yes | \./\.github/actions/toolchain | -     | The Go vulnerability database checker for the audit gate, installed by the same action. Queries the public vuln DB over the network; no credential.
docker         | command:docker       | yes | -                    | -     | A working Docker daemon for the image-build gate. Provided by the ubuntu-latest runner image, so no workflow step is required, but CI=true makes the gate mandatory, so a runner without Docker fails it.
jq             | command:jq           | yes | -                    | -     | `jq` for the driver-fixtures gate: scripts/driver/reader-verdict.sh parses the reader's verdict file with it, and its fixtures run in gates/driver.test.sh. Runner-provided, mandatory under CI=true.
process-repo   | env:XAL_PROCESS_DIR  | yes | XAL_PROCESS_DIR      | -     | A checkout of the process repo (factory.process_repo) plus XAL_PROCESS_DIR pointing at it, for the vendored-spec drift gate. The process repo is public, so the checkout needs no credential. THIS IS THE INPUT SHAPE THAT CAUSED THE ORIGINAL OUTAGE.
#
# THE PIPELINE'S ROWS (ADR-0007 and ADR-0008 in the factory; seeded with the driver, the
# chain, the merge and the reader). ONE ROW PER CONSUMER WORKFLOW, AND WHY THAT IS RIGHT: a
# workflow-scoped pattern names exactly one workflow, and the checker asserts that THAT file
# exists and supplies the secret. So a credential with four consumers needs four rows, and
# the manifest fails if any one of them loses the secret or the workflow disappears. One row
# would assert one consumer and quietly say nothing about the others. Every date below is a
# placeholder that fails gate 0 until a person writes the real expiry; the seeder prints
# this as a human-only step, and `xal-factory doctor` lists every placeholder left.
claude-oauth-driver | env:CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN | yes | driver.yml:secrets.CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN | REPLACE-WITH-THE-TOKEN-EXPIRY | The Claude GitHub App OAuth token that runs a plan session headless in `.github/workflows/driver.yml`. Not an input to scripts/check.sh, hence the workflow-scoped pattern. These tokens last one year from minting; write the mint date plus one year, and if it was minted before it was set here the real date is EARLIER, so re-issue rather than trust the arithmetic.
claude-oauth-reader | env:CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN | yes | reader.yml:secrets.CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN | REPLACE-WITH-THE-TOKEN-EXPIRY | The same token, consumed by `.github/workflows/reader.yml` to run the two-axis reader on every session PR. Same expiry derivation.
read-token-driver | env:FACTORY_READ_TOKEN | yes | driver.yml:secrets.FACTORY_READ_TOKEN | REPLACE-WITH-THE-TOKEN-EXPIRY | A token with READ on the ops repo (factory.ops_repo), consumed by `.github/workflows/driver.yml` to fetch the admitted spec a session is built against and verify it against the digest in docs/plan/plan.md. Given to the WORKFLOW and never to the agent. If the ops repo is public, any token with public read satisfies it.
read-token-reader | env:FACTORY_READ_TOKEN | yes | reader.yml:secrets.FACTORY_READ_TOKEN | REPLACE-WITH-THE-TOKEN-EXPIRY | The same read token, consumed by `.github/workflows/reader.yml` to fetch the admitted spec (digest-verified by fetch-spec.sh, exactly as the driver does) and the reader's definition from the public process repo at its pinned tag. Given to the workflow step, never to the agent.
write-token-driver | env:FACTORY_WRITE_TOKEN | yes | driver.yml:secrets.FACTORY_WRITE_TOKEN | REPLACE-WITH-THE-TOKEN-EXPIRY | A token with contents and pull-request WRITE on this repo and contents write on the ops repo, consumed by `.github/workflows/driver.yml` to OPEN the session pull request, PUSH the branch and append to the spend ledger. The PR and the push must be the same credential or the PR head goes unjudged, and `scripts/driver/step-order.sh` refuses a driver where they differ. GITHUB_TOKEN cannot open the PR without the repo setting that also lets Actions APPROVE pull requests, which must not exist under auto-merge, and a GITHUB_TOKEN-opened PR never triggers ci.yml.
write-token-chain | env:FACTORY_WRITE_TOKEN | yes | chain.yml:secrets.FACTORY_WRITE_TOKEN | REPLACE-WITH-THE-TOKEN-EXPIRY | The same token, consumed by `.github/workflows/chain.yml` for four things GITHUB_TOKEN cannot do: read the gate verdict on `main` before chaining onto it, read and write the spend ledger in the ops repo, DISPATCH driver.yml (a workflow dispatched with GITHUB_TOKEN does not run at all, which fails silently, the worst failure for an unattended chain), and open the escalation pull request.
write-token-merge | env:FACTORY_WRITE_TOKEN | yes | merge.yml:secrets.FACTORY_WRITE_TOKEN | REPLACE-WITH-THE-TOKEN-EXPIRY | The same token, consumed by `.github/workflows/merge.yml` to read CI's verdict and the reader's status on a session PR, bring the head up to date with main, MERGE it, and file the escalation issue in the ops repo. A GITHUB_TOKEN merge triggers no workflow, so chain.yml would never advance; merge-check rule 6 refuses that shape.
write-token-reader | env:FACTORY_WRITE_TOKEN | yes | reader.yml:secrets.FACTORY_WRITE_TOKEN | REPLACE-WITH-THE-TOKEN-EXPIRY | The same token, consumed by `.github/workflows/reader.yml` for ONE thing: appending the reader's spend to the ledger in the ops repo, so both ceilings see what reading a session cost. Used in a step that runs after the agent step has ended; the reader agent never holds it.
